# Line endings are a portability question, not a style preference.
#
# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true, so a clone there writes CRLF into the
# working tree, and a contributor whose config differs can commit those carriage returns
# straight back into the repository. The damage lands on Linux, where a trailing carriage
# return is part of the command: a shebang becomes "bad interpreter" and a shell line
# becomes $'\r': command not found. Declaring it here settles it once, for every machine,
# instead of depending on each developer's configuration being right.

# Whatever a platform wrote, text is stored normalised as LF in the repository. This is the
# rule that actually closes the hole: it is what stops carriage returns from being committed
# in the first place.
* text=auto

# Read or executed by Linux, so these have to be LF in the working tree as well, not only
# inside the repository.

# Invoked as ./scripts/*.sh by the README and by the CI; a carriage return breaks the
# shebang and every command line after it.
*.sh         text eol=lf

# .github/workflows/ci.yml embeds multi-line `run:` blocks that the runner hands to bash,
# and docker-compose.yml supplies environment values to containers. YAML itself tolerates
# CRLF; the shell and the values inside it do not.
*.yml        text eol=lf
*.yaml       text eol=lf

# Shell-form RUN lines are passed to /bin/sh -c inside the image, carriage return included.
Dockerfile   text eol=lf

# Copied to .env and read by docker compose. A carriage return here lands inside a value —
# the JWT secret, for instance — and fails as if the credential were wrong.
.env.example text eol=lf

# The only binary assets in the repository. Auto-detection already gets these right; saying
# so explicitly means a checkout never depends on that heuristic.
*.png binary
